<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!-- For BLE beacon auto check-in (see AccountSettingsScreen's Auto Check-In
         toggle).

         BLUETOOTH_SCAN deliberately does NOT carry usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation",
         even though that flag reads as exactly right for us: we only ever match one known
         beacon UUID and never derive a physical location. The catch is that Android keeps
         a "location denylist" of advertising data patterns, and iBeacon and Eddystone
         payloads are on it by design, since beacons are the canonical way to derive
         location from BLE. Any app that disavows location via that flag gets those
         advertisements silently stripped from its scan results. On device this looked like
         every other BLE device arriving normally while the club beacon alone never showed
         up, with one "BtScan.ScanController: Location deny list ... Skip" logged per
         advertisement. Setting the flag would filter out the only advertisement the
         feature exists to see.

         Scanning therefore counts as location capable, which is why ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION
         is requested for real (no maxSdkVersion cap) and prompted at runtime alongside the
         Bluetooth permissions, and why the prompts say Precise explicitly.

         Whether the platform would in fact deliver scan results on an Approximate only
         grant has never actually been measured, and an earlier note here claiming it had
         been was wrong. The app refuses to scan without Precise, so the one test run
         measured that refusal rather than anything Android did. Since BLE scanning has
         wanted ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION since Android 10 the requirement is very likely real,
         but treat it as assumed. Worth measuring if the size of the permission ask ever
         becomes a problem, by relaxing the gate in AccountSettingsScreen and
         AutoCheckinController and watching whether advertisements still arrive.

         Verified separately, and this part was measured: only FINE is declared here, the
         platform adds COARSE implicitly, and permission handler requests just what the
         manifest declares, so Permission.location reports denied on an Approximate only
         grant. The toggle refuses to switch on rather than enabling and silently never
         detecting anything.

         BeaconScanner still never reads device location; it only parses manufacturer data
         bytes out of the advertisements it receives. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <!-- Deliberately NOT requesting ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION. Walk in
         detection works without it: the scan registered with the OS
         (BeaconScanControl) is delivered to BeaconScanReceiver even with the
         app force killed and that permission revoked, verified on device by
         swiping the app away and then powering the beacon on, which cold
         started the process to deliver the broadcast. The registered scan
         belongs to the system rather than to us, which appears to be why it
         escapes the background location gate that a self held scan would hit.

         An earlier round of testing seemed to show the opposite, but that was
         measuring a scan configured with CALLBACK_TYPE_FIRST_MATCH, which
         this hardware never reports on at all; the permission was never the
         variable. Worth re testing before anyone adds it back, since it costs
         members an "Allow all the time" prompt and a Play Store review. -->

    <!-- For the Hosting tab's "new pending cash payment" local notification
         (NotificationService). Required on Android 13+ for the runtime
         permission request to have anything to grant. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <application
        android:label="TGG Mobile"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Target of the PendingIntent handed to BluetoothLeScanner for
             background beacon detection (BeaconScanControl). The system
             delivers scan results here even when the app has no running
             process, starting one if needed, which is what allows walk in
             check in without a foreground service. Not exported: the only
             legitimate sender is the platform's own Bluetooth stack. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".BeaconScanReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
